{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966814,"nonce":"1HcZBkpP8U00os9fkidemwwGy4Q=","title":"Blinc reactive architecture v2 — unified property channel, signal-bound modifiers,","message":"Synthesis of the Vizia comparison ([[project-blinc-perf-vs-vizia]]), the current layout-tree stability substrate ([[project-layout-tree-stability-substrate]]), and the measured CPU hotspots (toast 40%, scroll 25%, slider 18%, switch 11%, spinner 13% @ 30fps).\n\n**Why:** Roadmap §1.5 component work keeps surfacing CPU floor — single animations cost 10-20%, simultaneous interaction stacks past 100%. Architectural rather than tactical fix needed to win on lean + fast + resource-friendly + visual fidelity simultaneously. Drafted as the *target* architecture; existing substrate (incremental_update, StableNodeId, queue_prop_update, apply_binding_deltas) is the foundation, not replaced.\n\n**How to apply:** When evaluating new features or perf work, check which phase below it belongs to. Don't sequence ahead — each phase ships value independently and assumes earlier ones. Don't propose work that conflicts with the unified-channel principle without explicit revision of this doc.\n\n---\n\n\nSingle unified property channel. Every visual-state mutation (signals, CSS state, CSS animations, transitions, motion bindings, pointer events, direct user calls) flows through one queue, drained once per frame. Source differentiation lives in metadata; processing is uniform.\n\n```\nLAYER A — Builder API\n  Same call sites: `.bg(v)`, `.w(v)`, etc.\n  Generic over `IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e` (eager T | \u0026State\u003cT\u003e | future computed closures).\n  Layout-affecting props with transform equivalents get `.animated_*` helpers\n  (transform_width, animated_scale_x).\n\nLAYER B — Reactive Sources\n  Signal subscriptions, CSS state changes, @keyframes ticks, transitions,\n  motion bindings (spring/timeline), pointer-driven, direct user.\n  Each emits `PropertyChange { target, prop, value, source_meta }`.\n\nLAYER C — Property Registry\n  PropertyId enum with side-effect metadata (needs_layout, affects_clip,\n  animatable, transform_equivalent).\n  BindingRegistry: HashMap\u003cSignalId, Vec\u003c(LayoutNodeId, PropertyId, FieldWriter)\u003e\u003e\n  StateStyleTable: HashMap\u003c(StableNodeId, ElementState), Vec\u003c(PropId, Value)\u003e\u003e\n    — pre-resolved CSS cascade, never per-frame stylesheet walk.\n  AnimationRegistry: deadline-bucketed (time-wheel), not flat HashMap.\n  MotionRegistry: tagged with transform-only flag for texture-cache promotion.\n\nLAYER D — Unified Update Queue\n  Single Vec\u003cPropertyChange\u003e drained per frame.\n  Dedup by (target, prop) — latest wins.\n  Side-effect classification (needs_layout, needs_text_remeasure).\n  Quantize-equal skip at drain (don't enqueue noop).\n\nLAYER E — Apply Pipeline\n  write into RenderProps[node].field  (Phase 1 storage shape)\n       OR PropertyStore.field.insert(node, v)  (Phase 9 shape, optional)\n  write to taffy Style if needs_layout.\n  Unified `apply_property_deltas` folds binding/css/state into one pass.\n\nLAYER F — Compositor\n  Static cache TILED (~256px strips); scroll invalidates per-strip.\n  Motion-bound subtree overlay → bakes to GPU texture when transform/opacity\n  only; animates the texture, doesn't re-rasterize subtree.\n  Dynamic batch for per-frame mutations.\n  Damage-rect dispatch over text/SVG/image (no longer SDF-only).\n\nLAYER G — GPU Submission\n  Quantize-equal skip (sub-pixel pos, 8-bit colour, 1° rotation).\n  Coalesced encoder: one upload + one dispatch per frame regardless of N bindings.\n\nCross-cutting (feeders / lifecycle):\n  • Pointer-move coalescing at event router → Layer B\n  • Animation deadline scheduler → Layer B\n  • Spring visual-settle detection (separate from physical) → Layer B exit\n  • Subtree-as-texture promotion at motion-start → Layer F\n```\n\n\n1. **The channel is the unification point.** One queue, one drain, one apply pipeline. Differentiation lives in source metadata.\n2. **Identity preserved everywhere** via existing layout-stability substrate. LayoutNodeId for in-place patches, StableNodeId for structural rebuilds.\n3. **Cost proportional to changed properties, not registered properties.** State table, deadline buckets, per-tile invalidation, quantize-skip, value-changed bit.\n4. **Visual fidelity preserved by reducing wasted work** — subtree-as-texture, damage-rect, scale-x-vs-width all produce identical visual output at fraction of CPU.\n5. **API surface stable** for the common case. Generic builders accept eager or reactive transparently. Animation-friendly helpers are additive.\n\n---\n\n\nTotal: ~9 months for P1–P8 focused effort. P9 optional (~2 months) after measurement.\n\n**Status:** in-progress (started 2026-05-24, main branch)\n**Estimate:** 4 weeks\n**Risk:** low\n\n**Substep 1.1 — Foundation plumbing (shipped `ca648e91`, 2026-05-24)**\n\n- `blinc_layout::property` module: `PropertyId` enum (visual + layout + text + Compound), `SideEffects { needs_layout, needs_text_remeasure, affects_clip }`, `TransformEquivalent` lookup.\n- `queue_prop_update_partial(node_id, prop, effects, closure)` + `PENDING_PARTIAL_PROP_UPDATES` static + drain pair (`take_pending_partial_prop_updates`, `has_pending_partial_prop_updates`).\n- All four platform runners (windowed, web, android, ios) drain the partial queue and OR accumulated `effects.needs_layout` into the existing relayout gate.\n- `tools/blinc-regression` skeleton — 16-scenario catalogue keyed to cn_demo sections + phase targets. CLI shape (`baseline` / `compare` / `list`) wired; capture step stubbed pending later substeps.\n- Tests: 6 new in `property::tests`; full workspace clippy `-D warnings` clean; 660 `blinc_layout` tests unchanged.\n\n**Substep 1.2 — Collapse the dual-queue plumbing (shipped, see commit list below)**\n\n- `queue_prop_update(node_id, props)` is now a backward-compat shim that routes through the unified property channel as `PropertyId::Compound` + `SideEffects::VISUAL`. No callers changed; behaviour preserved.\n- `PENDING_PROP_UPDATES` static and `take_pending_prop_updates` removed entirely. One queue, one drain pair.\n- All five platform-runner drains simplified: each calls `take_pending_partial_prop_updates` once and accumulates side effects from there. Pre-migration dual-loop deleted.\n- `apply_binding_deltas`, `apply_css_deltas`, and animation-driven `queue_prop_update` callers continue to work without further changes — they hit the shim.\n\n**Future micro-improvements** (deferred — not blocking subsequent phases):\n- The three remaining `queue_prop_update` call sites (`stateful.rs` × 2, `selector/handle.rs` × 1) could emit narrower `PropertyId`s in the few places where the diff is field-known. Not worth the audit cost yet — `Compound` is correct and the cost only shows up in P5+ work where per-property classification starts mattering for the CSS state table.\n\n**Substep 1.3 — CPU-sampling capture step (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\n- `blinc-regression record --out FILE [-- CMD ARGS...]` spawns a subprocess (default: cn_demo release) and samples its CPU% every 100ms via `sysinfo`. Default command: `cargo run -p blinc_app_examples --example cn_demo --release --features cn`. On subprocess exit, writes a JSON timeseries with per-sample CPU% + warmup flag + summary stats (mean, p50, p95, max, n) over the post-warmup window.\n- `blinc-regression compare --baseline F1 --after F2 [--phase N]` reads two recordings and prints absolute + relative CPU% deltas. With `--phase`, echoes the scenarios that phase exercises so the human knows which numbers should have moved.\n- Sampling is from outside (subprocess CPU% via sysinfo); cn_demo / blinc_app are not modified.\n- Warmup window = first 1500ms (cargo build + rustc startup dominate). Post-warmup summary is the headline number; with-warmup summary kept for reference.\n- Smoke-tested against `sleep 2` (0% CPU as expected) and a Python busy-loop subprocess (~50% on test machine).\n\n**Deferred to a later substep:** visual screenshot capture + diff. Adds engineering scope (renderer-to-PNG path + image-compare crate) that isn't blocking P2. Wire when first visual regression is suspected.\n\n**Substep 1.4 — Scenario-driven automation (deferred — not blocking).**\nProgrammatic input injection (slider drag, hover sweep, etc.) would let `record` run the catalogue without human intervention. Platform-specific (X11 / Cocoa / Wayland), and the current human-driven flow is reproducible enough for phase validation. Pick up if subsequent phases need overnight regression runs.\n\n**Win:** ~1-3% CPU on busy interaction (drain dedup + side-effect batching). Mostly sets the rails.\n\n**Measured (Substep 1.1):** no CPU change — purely additive plumbing, no existing callers migrated yet.\n\n**Status:** in-progress (started 2026-05-24)\n**Estimate:** 6 weeks\n**Risk:** medium\n\n**Substep breakdown:**\n- 2.1 — `IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e` trait + `BindingRegistry` data structure + `State::set` hook substrate (~1 week)\n- 2.2 — Migrate `.bg()` + `.opacity()` as proof-of-concept; wire one cn component (~1 week)\n- 2.3 — Roll out across remaining Tier-1 visual properties (border_color, transform, shadow, color, filter, accent_color, corner_radius) (~2 weeks)\n- 2.4 — Tier-2 layout properties (w / h / padding / margin / gap / flex_*) with needs_layout side-effect (~1.5 weeks)\n- 2.5 — Compile-time impact audit + type-erased internal stub if needed (~0.5 weeks)\n\n**Substep 2.4 — Tier-2 layout properties (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\nLayout props (`.w` / `.h` / `.padding` / `.gap`) now signal-bindable via the same `impl IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e` surface. Targets the live taffy `Style` through a parallel write channel; signal updates trigger relayout the next frame.\n\n- `PartialPropertyUpdate` refactored: `write: Box\u003c...\u003e` field replaced with two `Option\u003cBox\u003c...\u003e\u003e` fields — `render_write` (Tier-1) + `layout_write` (Tier-2). Either or both may be `Some`. New type aliases `RenderPropsWrite` and `TaffyStyleWrite`.\n- New entry point: `queue_layout_update_partial(node_id, property, effects, write)` parallel to `queue_prop_update_partial`. Same lazy-init notifier hookup, same drain queue (single Vec, dispatch by which field is Some).\n- All five platform-runner drains (windowed, web ×2, android, ios) updated to handle both writes:\n  - If `render_write` is Some → existing `tree.update_render_props(...)` path.\n  - If `layout_write` is Some → read current taffy style via `tree.layout_tree.get_style(...)`, mutate via closure, write back via `set_style(...)`. Read-modify-write because taffy's API is setter-only — `~µs` round-trip per update, acceptable for the workload.\n- `binding.rs` substrate extended:\n  - `LayoutWriteFn` type alias parallel to `WriteFn`.\n  - `SubscriberWrite` enum dispatched in `fire` — Render goes to `queue_prop_update_partial`, Layout to `queue_layout_update_partial`.\n  - `PropertyBindingRegistry::register_layout(...)` parallel to `register(...)`.\n  - `register_typed_layout\u003cT\u003e(...)` convenience.\n  - `LayoutPendingBinding\u003cT\u003e` implementing `PendingBinding` — same shape as `TypedPendingBinding` but targets the layout queue.\n- Four `Div` builder methods migrated to `impl IntoReactive\u003cf32\u003e`:\n  - `.w(...)`  → `PropertyId::Width`\n  - `.h(...)`  → `PropertyId::Height`\n  - `.gap(...)` → `PropertyId::Gap`\n  - `.p(...)`  → `PropertyId::Padding`\n- Each carries `SideEffects { needs_layout: true, ... }` via the PropertyId's metadata; drain accumulator OR's into the per-frame `needs_layout` flag → `compute_layout` runs next frame.\n- 4 new integration tests covering `.w` / `.h` / `.gap` / `.p` end-to-end. Each verifies: layout_write is Some, render_write is None, effects.needs_layout is true, and applying the layout_write to taffy::Style produces the expected new dimension. 18 binding tests total pass.\n- cn_demo `signal_bound_modifier_section` extended with width row (Width 80 / 120 / 240). The swatch now binds five properties concurrently: `.w(\u0026width).h(120).bg(\u0026bg).opacity(\u0026op).rounded(\u0026radius).border(2.0, transparent).border_color(\u0026bc)`. Width changes trigger relayout; the others are pure cell patches.\n- 678 blinc_layout tests + 85 blinc_core tests + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + rustdoc clean.\n\n**Deferred from P2.4 scope:**\n- `.margin` + `.flex_direction` / `.flex_grow` / `.flex_shrink` / `.flex_wrap` / `.align_items` / `.justify_content` — same pattern, just more migrations. Land in P2.4b if needed for component work; the four shipped cover ~90% of practical animated-layout usage.\n- Helper `.p_px` / `.px(units)` / `.py(units)` / `.w_full` / `.w_auto` / `.w_fit` — they take fixed values or no args; reactive variants don't make semantic sense.\n- The 4 px Tailwind-unit multiplier is encoded inside the bound closure for `.p` / `.gap` — `state.set(4.0)` produces 16 px. Matches the eager API. Pixel-precise control still available via the per-axis builders (not migrated yet).\n\n**Measured (Substep 2.4):** unmeasured — width row in cn_demo is the natural exercise. Per-frame layout cost during a width-button click is small (single-node taffy recompute on a small subtree). The architectural cost reduction shows up when a *Stateful*-wrapped width-animated subtree migrates to `.w(\u0026signal)` — the rebuild cost goes from O(subtree) to O(1).\n\n**Substep 2.3 — Roll out across remaining Tier-1 visual properties (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\nBaseline captured at `baselines/20260524-123201Z-before-p2-3.json` (mean 8.08%, p95 22.46%, max 46.96% over 14s).\n\n- Migrated four Div builder methods to `impl IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e`:\n  - `.rounded(impl IntoReactive\u003cf32\u003e)` — PropertyId::CornerRadius\n  - `.border_color(impl IntoReactive\u003cColor\u003e)` — PropertyId::BorderColor\n  - `.shadow(impl IntoReactive\u003cShadow\u003e)` — PropertyId::Shadow\n  - `.transform(impl IntoReactive\u003cTransform\u003e)` — PropertyId::Transform\n- Each follows the P2.2 pattern: Const branch sets directly, Bound branch seeds initial value + queues a `TypedPendingBinding` with the property-specific write closure. Eager call sites unchanged (workspace builds clean with no migration in cn / examples).\n- 4 new integration tests in `binding::tests` covering each migrated prop end-to-end. All 14 binding tests pass (10 from P2.1/P2.2 + 4 new).\n- cn_demo's `signal_bound_modifier_section` extended with radius (4 / 16 / 60) + border-color (dark / cyan / magenta) buttons. Swatch is now `.bg(\u0026bg).opacity(\u0026op).rounded(\u0026radius).border(2.0, transparent).border_color(\u0026bc)` — four concurrent signal bindings on one element, no Stateful in sight.\n- 674 blinc_layout tests + 85 blinc_core tests + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + rustdoc clean.\n\n**Deferred from P2.3 scope:**\n- `.outline_color` + `.cursor` — visual-only but no existing PropertyId enum entries. Add when a real consumer needs them.\n- `.filter` (layer_effects) — `Vec\u003cLayerEffect\u003e` shape doesn't fit single-value IntoReactive cleanly. Wants a List-binding variant; defer.\n- `color` and `accent_color` — text-element-specific (not on Div). Migration belongs in the per-Text / per-component work, not on Div.\n- Transform helpers (`.translate`, `.rotate`, `.scale`) — they compose into `Transform`. Could expose `Transform::translate(x, y)` constructions as reactive but it's cleaner for callers to use `.transform(\u0026state)` directly with a `State\u003cTransform\u003e`. Skipped for clarity.\n\n**Measured (Substep 2.3):** unmeasured — capture `--label after-p2-3` against the new cn_demo section to attribute the swatch demo's CPU. Most of cn_demo is untouched eager call sites, so the global CPU delta will be in the noise; the win surfaces in scenarios that actively exercise the migrated paths.\n\n\n\n**Substep 2.2 — `.bg()` + `.opacity()` proof-of-concept (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\n- `Reactive\u003cT\u003e` simplified: `{ Const(T) | Bound(State\u003cT\u003e) }`. The old read/write closure pair on the variant moved to per-call-site impls; the user-facing enum just carries the data source.\n- `IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e` impls:\n  - `impl\u003cT\u003e IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e for T` — eager (blanket).\n  - `impl\u003cT: Clone + Send + 'static\u003e IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e for \u0026State\u003cT\u003e` — most common reactive form.\n  - `impl\u003cT: Clone + Send + 'static\u003e IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e for State\u003cT\u003e` — owned-state convenience.\n- New `PendingBinding` trait + `TypedPendingBinding\u003cT\u003e` concrete impl in `binding.rs`. Element builders hold `Vec\u003cBox\u003cdyn PendingBinding\u003e\u003e` and drain it in their `build()` to register against the minted `LayoutNodeId`.\n- `Div` migrated:\n  - New `pending_bindings` field, threaded through both constructors + `Div::merge` (appended, not replaced — both sides keep their subscriptions).\n  - `Div::build` calls `binding.register(node)` for each pending binding right after children are wired.\n  - `.bg()` and `.opacity()` now generic over `impl IntoReactive\u003c…\u003e`. Eager call sites (`div().bg(Color::RED)`, `div().opacity(0.5)`) compile unchanged. Signal-bound call sites (`div().bg(\u0026state)`, `div().opacity(\u0026state)`) register subscriptions.\n- Demo: new `signal_bound_modifier_section(ctx)` in `cn_demo.rs` — purple/cyan/magenta swatch driven by `State\u003cColor\u003e`, opacity buttons driven by `State\u003cf32\u003e`. No `Stateful` wrapper around the swatch. Provides the visual baseline for measuring P2 wins vs. an equivalent Stateful-wrapped color-change.\n- 4 new integration tests in `binding::tests`: `into_reactive_bound_path_from_state_ref`, `div_bg_eager_path_unchanged`, `div_bg_bound_path_fires_on_state_set`, `div_opacity_bound_path_fires_on_state_set`. Plus the 7 from 2.1 — all 10 binding tests green.\n- 670 blinc_layout tests + 85 blinc_core tests + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + rustdoc clean.\n\n**Measured (Substep 2.2):** unmeasured — requires running `record` over the new demo section with + without the bound modifiers as the comparison point. The cn_demo signal-bound section is the regression bed; capture baseline + diff will land alongside the next phase comparison.\n\n**Substep 2.1 — Foundation (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\n- `blinc_core::reactive` gains a process-global `set_property_binding_notifier(impl Fn(SignalId))` hook (OnceLock). `State\u003cT\u003e::set` / `set_rebuild` / `update` / `update_rebuild` all call `notify_property_bindings(id)` after the value update. No-op when no notifier is installed.\n- `blinc_layout::binding` module ships the registry + trait substrate:\n  - `Reactive\u003cT\u003e { Const(T) | Bound { signal_id, read, write } }` — what `IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e` resolves to.\n  - `BoundValue` — type-erased `Box\u003cdyn Any + Send + Sync\u003e` for shuttling values through the registry without `T` leaking into the storage layer.\n  - `IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e` trait, with blanket-impl `impl\u003cT\u003e IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e for T` shipping the eager-value path. Bound path lands in P2.2 via per-State-reference impls.\n  - `PropertyBindingRegistry` with `register / unregister_node / fire / signal_count / subscriber_count`. Two-way HashMap keyed by signal_id and node_id so node-eviction is O(per-binding-on-that-node).\n  - `register_typed(signal_id, node_id, property, state, write)` convenience that wraps the type-erasure boilerplate.\n  - Process-global `REGISTRY: LazyLock\u003cMutex\u003c...\u003e\u003e` whose initialiser installs the core notifier — no init order coupling for platform runners.\n- `remove_subtree_nodes` ([build/subtree.rs:175](crates/blinc_layout/src/renderer/build/subtree.rs#L175)) calls `binding::unregister_node(node_id)` so structurally-removed nodes can't fire stale bindings.\n- 5 tests in `binding::tests` covering: const-path, register-then-fire-queues-update, unregister-stops-firing, multiple-subscribers-one-signal, partial-unregister-preserves-others. Tests serialised via a static `Mutex` because they share the global registry and reset it per-test (each fresh `ReactiveGraph` restarts signal-id counter at 0, so collisions across parallel tests are real).\n- Workspace builds + clippy `-D warnings` clean. 665 blinc_layout tests + 85 blinc_core tests green.\n\n- `IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e` trait dispatched in every Div builder method.\n- `BindingRegistry: HashMap\u003cSignalId, Vec\u003c(LayoutNodeId, PropertyId, FieldWriter)\u003e\u003e` on RenderTree.\n- `State\u003cT\u003e::set` hook → walk registry → queue per-property updates via Phase 1 channel.\n- `remove_subtree_nodes` evicts bindings.\n- Compile-time impact mitigation (typed-erased internal stub if needed).\n- Migrate 2-3 cn components to prove the path.\n\n**Risk drivers:** Generic explosion across builder methods may bloat compile time; need prototyping on a branch first.\n**Win:** 10-30% CPU reduction on state-change-heavy scenes (forms, hover storms). Closes the \"state change → property change\" bucket. Largest single architectural win for *non-animation* cost.\n\n**Status:** in-progress (started 2026-05-24); P3.1a shipped + measured; P3.2 mostly pre-shipped (text/SVG/image wired May 17) — default-on blocked on Metal stutter\n**Estimate:** 3 weeks (revised down — much of P3.2 turned out to be already done)\n**Risk:** medium\n\n**Substep breakdown:**\n- 3.1 — Pointer-move event coalescing (~1 week). Event router accumulates pointer-move events between vsync ticks; dispatch latest position once per frame. Drag fast path keeps correct latest-position semantics.\n- 3.2 — Damage-rect finish for text / SVG / image. **Mostly pre-shipped** as commits `fb511fdf` (scaffolding) + `1608a571` (Phase 3.B — text/SVG/image dispatch via `pending_scissor`). The infrastructure is complete: `GpuRenderer::pending_scissor` setter, `glyphs_by_layer` cache, scissored re-dispatch through existing `render_text` / `render_images` paths. **What's left:** Metal animation stutter investigation. cn_demo switch toggle with `BLINC_DAMAGE_RECT=1` shows mid-transition jitter the slow path doesn't exhibit; suspect partial `LoadOp::Load` + scissored draw composition cost on Metal vs. slow path's `LoadOp::Clear` + full draw. Default-off until this clears. See [[gotcha-damage-rect-clears-text]].\n\n**Substep 3.1a — Recency-gate coalescing (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\nMinimal-viable sub-frame coalescing without the full Moved-arm refactor. The full per-vsync coalescing (buffer + drain at frame start) requires extracting the 200+ line Moved-arm dispatch into a helper that both the event path and the Frame arm can invoke — meaningful scope. Recency gate is a smaller landable atom that captures most of the win.\n\n- New `WindowState.last_pointer_dispatch: Option\u003cInstant\u003e`.\n- Insertion in windowed.rs Moved-arm, after the existing fast-path early-returns (bare-move static UI / cursor-only) and before the heavy hit-test + dispatch.\n- Gate: if `last_pointer_dispatch.elapsed() \u003c 8ms` AND no press in flight AND no `POINTER_MOVE` subscriber on the tree, skip the heavy dispatch. Inline updates: cursor style + `router.set_mouse_position` + `clear_event_callback`. Frame-dirty + redraw not flipped (no functional change happened).\n- After the heavy dispatch runs (drag-fast or move-with-occlusion), stamp `last_pointer_dispatch = Some(Instant::now())`.\n- **Conservative**: 8 ms is half a 60 Hz frame. At 1000 Hz mouse rate, ~16 dispatches/frame → ~2. At 144 Hz display + 1000 Hz mouse, ~7 → 1. The \"user moves fast then stops exactly mid-window\" edge case leaves hover state up to 8 ms stale until the next event — half a 60 Hz frame, imperceptible.\n- **Skipped on purpose**:\n  - Press-in-flight (drag handlers may track sub-frame deltas)\n  - POINTER_MOVE subscribers (continuous-tracking handlers like scroll-velocity)\n  - Pointer-query consumers (flow shaders / calc(env(...))) — already handled by the short-circuit earlier in the arm\n- 678 blinc_layout tests + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + rustdoc clean. No tests broken; no API change.\n\n**Substep 3.1b — full per-vsync coalescing (deferred)**\n\nFor the scenarios where 3.1a's 8 ms window still misses dispatches (e.g. user moves slowly), the full design would:\n- Buffer the latest `(lx, ly)` in `pending_mouse_move`\n- Defer all dispatch (even the first per-frame one) to the start of `Event::Frame`\n- Refactor the Moved-arm dispatch into a helper invokable from both arms\n\nScope: extracting that helper is ~200 lines of careful refactor with intricate dependencies on overlay_manager, hover_invalidation, frame_dirty, request_redraw, drag_delta tracking, and pending_events scaffolding. Pick up if 3.1a's measured impact is short of the envelope projection.\n\n**Win projection (Substep 3.1a):** Slider drag 18% → ~14-15% (closer to 13% with full coalescing). Spinner unaffected (no pointer moves). Hover-storm-buttons should benefit similarly to slider drag.\n\n**Measured (Substep 3.1a, after hotfix):**\n- Captures: `baselines/20260524-125837Z-after-p2-4.json` (baseline, 11min, 6176 samples) → `baselines/20260524-135648Z-after-p3-1a.json` (after, 8.3min, 4708 samples).\n- aggregate cn_demo: **p95 -19.0% (20.47% → 16.58%)**, mean -27.3% (3.67% → 2.67%), max -3.9% (52.50% → 50.48%), p50 unchanged at 0%.\n- p95 is the duration-resilient metric and the headline number — confirms hover-driven scenarios pay materially less for continuous pointer activity.\n- mean has duration-mismatch noise (8.3min vs 11min) but moves the right direction.\n- max drop suggests even the heaviest interactions benefit slightly (overlay enter + concurrent hover motion is the likely scenario).\n- **First measurable win on cn_demo from v2 work.** P2.1-2.4 substrate didn't move cn_demo numbers (cn widgets unmigrated); P3.1a intercepts existing widget interactions without needing migration → pure compounding-leverage spot.\n- Hotfix (commit `e108c06b`): the original 3.1a used `get_cursor_at` in the skip path, which runs a fresh hit_test → burned most of the savings. Swapped to `get_cursor_for_last_hit` (cached ancestor chain walk). First measurement showed regression; post-fix shows the real win.\n\n- Event router accumulates pointer-move between vsync ticks; dispatches once per frame.\n- Drag-fast-path stays correct (latest-position semantics).\n- Finish damage-rect for text/SVG/image (existing gated `BLINC_DAMAGE_RECT` scaffolding, see [[gotcha-damage-rect-clears-text]]).\n- Default-on once finished.\n\n**Risk drivers:** Coalescing changes event ordering — careful with drag handlers that snapshot pointer state per event.\n**Win:** Slider 18% → ~13%. Spinner 13% → ~9%. Every drag-driven interaction benefits proportionally.\n\n**Status:** in-progress (started 2026-05-24); P4.1 + P4.2 shipped\n**Estimate:** 5 weeks\n**Risk:** high\n\n**Substep breakdown:**\n- 4.1 — Detection pass (shipped). Walker marks subtree roots whose only active animation source is transform / opacity AND no descendant carries an independent animation. Foundation only — no consumer.\n- 4.2 — Texture-baking infrastructure (shipped). GPU-agnostic `MotionSubtreeBakeRegistry` on RenderTree tracks Pending → Baked → Invalidated lifecycle per candidate. Auto-demotes lapsed records on the same compute pass that updates the candidate set. P4.3 plugs the GPU bake call into this lifecycle; the existing `LayerTextureCache` (used by CSS composite-promotion) already provides the offscreen render pass + pooled-texture allocator.\n- 4.3 — Bake-at-motion-start hook + per-frame texture-blit during motion. Wires `WindowedContext.motion_subtree_textures: HashMap\u003cLayoutNodeId, LayerTexture\u003e` (mirrors existing `css_composited_textures`), the walker gate on `subtree_texture_candidates`, the end-of-paint `render_subtree_to_layer_texture` call → `mark_motion_subtree_baked`, and the blit-emit in the compositor's overlay pass.\n- 4.4 — Invalidation triggers (structural rebuild, non-transform binding fires, descendant CSS animation toggles, bounds change). Each call site invokes `invalidate_motion_subtree_bake` on the relevant ancestor candidate.\n- 4.5 — LRU eviction policy + memory cap (reuses `LayerTextureCache.evict_oversized` + adds a hard byte cap across `motion_subtree_textures` + `css_composited_textures`).\n\n**Substep 4.2 — Texture-baking infrastructure (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\n- New `blinc_layout::motion_texture_cache` module ships:\n  - `MotionBakeState { Pending, Baked, Invalidated }` — lifecycle states.\n  - `MotionSubtreeBakeRecord { bounds, build_generation, state }` — per-subtree bookkeeping.\n  - `MotionSubtreeBakeRegistry` — `HashMap\u003cLayoutNodeId, MotionSubtreeBakeRecord\u003e` wrapper with `prepare / mark_baked / invalidate / remove / get / len / iter / demote_lapsed`.\n- New `RenderTree` field `motion_subtree_bake_registry: RefCell\u003cMotionSubtreeBakeRegistry\u003e` + 6 inherent lifecycle methods (`prepare_motion_subtree_bake / mark_motion_subtree_baked / invalidate_motion_subtree_bake / motion_subtree_bake_record / motion_subtree_bake_count / demote_lapsed_motion_bake_records`).\n- `compute_subtree_texture_candidates` calls `demote_lapsed_motion_bake_records` at the end (BOTH the early-return \"no animating roots\" path AND the normal path). The demote step drops records whose node is no longer a candidate — even if the GPU side hasn't been wired yet, this keeps the registry from accumulating stale entries during P4.3 development.\n- 9 module-level tests + 5 RenderTree-integration tests:\n  - `prepare_inserts_pending_record`, `prepare_idempotent_when_already_pending`, `mark_baked_flips_state_once`, `mark_baked_on_missing_node_is_noop`, `invalidate_flips_baked_to_invalidated`, `remove_drops_record`, `demote_lapsed_drops_only_non_candidates`, `demote_lapsed_empty_candidate_set_clears_registry`, `record_pending_helper_sets_state` (module-level).\n  - `motion_bake_registry_starts_empty`, `prepare_bake_inserts_pending_record_with_tree_generation`, `mark_bake_then_invalidate_state_transitions`, `demote_lapsed_runs_inside_compute_subtree_texture_candidates` (catches the early-return bypass!), `demote_lapsed_keeps_records_for_active_candidates` (RenderTree integration).\n- Public surface: `blinc_layout::{MotionBakeState, MotionSubtreeBakeRecord, MotionSubtreeBakeRegistry}` re-exported.\n- 712 blinc_layout tests + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + workspace rustdoc clean.\n\n**Why a pure-bookkeeping landing:** the CSS composite-promotion path already has the full GPU machinery (`LayerTexture`, `LayerTextureCache` with bucket-pool + LRU, `render_subtree_to_layer_texture`, blit shader). P4.2's job is the metadata layer that lets the walker decide per-frame whether to recurse-and-emit (Pending or Invalidated) or emit-a-blit (Baked). Separating the metadata from the GPU wiring keeps P4.3 small (~one walker gate, one bake-call site, one demote-callback hookup) and keeps P4.2 fully testable without spinning up a wgpu device.\n\n**Measured (Substep 4.2):** unmeasured by design — no callers yet, no GPU texture creation, no walker integration. Runtime cost is the registry's `compute_subtree_texture_candidates` demote pass: O(records) per frame, bounded by the actively-animating motion-bound roots — typically 0-3 in cn_demo scenarios.\n\n**Substep 4.1 — Detection pass (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\n- `RenderTree::compute_subtree_texture_candidates(\u0026self)` populates a new `RefCell\u003cHashSet\u003cLayoutNodeId\u003e\u003e` field with every node `R` satisfying both criteria simultaneously:\n  1. `R.motion_bindings.is_any_animating() == true` — there's a transform / opacity spring currently mid-flight on the root. (`MotionBindings` only exposes transform + opacity bindings, so the \"transform-or-opacity-only motion\" predicate is satisfied by definition when any root binding animates.)\n  2. Every descendant of `R` is clean: no own motion binding mid-flight, no `ElementType::Canvas` (unconditionally dynamic), no CSS keyframe / transition playing on its `StableNodeId`.\n- Detection runs as a side-effect of [`compute_animation_status`] — appended after the existing composite-promotion pass so every caller (`try_render_with_compositor` × 2 call sites) picks up both maps from one pass without wiring changes.\n- Algorithm gathers actively-animating roots first (filters out settled bindings), short-circuits to clear the candidate set when no root animates (common case), snapshots the CSS-anim store ONCE per pass (no per-descendant lock), then does iterative DFS over each animating root's layout-tree descendants with the triple disqualifier check.\n- Public surface: `subtree_texture_candidates() -\u003e Ref\u003cHashSet\u003cLayoutNodeId\u003e\u003e` + `is_subtree_texture_candidate(node) -\u003e bool`. Both gated as informational for P4.1; P4.2 / P4.3 are the first consumers.\n- Cost: O(animating_roots × avg_subtree_size). For typical scenarios (toast / drawer / dialog enter, switch thumb translate) the animating root count is 1-3 and subtree size dozens of nodes — sub-microsecond at the scale that matters.\n- 8 unit tests cover: no motion at all → empty set; settled spring → not a candidate; animating-transform root + clean subtree → candidate; opacity-only root → candidate; descendant motion binding disqualifies parent (but descendant qualifies independently); descendant CSS keyframe disqualifies; descendant CSS transition disqualifies; `compute_animation_status` populates the candidate set as a side-effect.\n- 694 blinc_layout tests + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + workspace rustdoc clean.\n\n**Behaviour note:** the candidate test does NOT exclude nested candidates. If `A` is a motion-bound root containing motion-bound descendant `B`, `A` is correctly rejected (descendant `B` is animating) but `B` is a candidate in its own right (its own subtree is clean). P4.2 / P4.3 may layer additional overlap policy when texture-baking actually happens.\n\n**Measured (Substep 4.1):** unmeasured — detection only; no GPU / paint path changes yet. The CPU win lands when P4.2 / P4.3 ship and the cached texture short-circuits per-frame subtree rasterization.\n\n**Risk drivers (P4.2-4.5):** Texture lifecycle correctness, GPU memory bounds, exhaustive subtree-invalidation triggers.\n**Win (cumulative across P4):** Toast 40% → ~6%. Drawer/sheet/dialog enter/exit benefit equivalently. cn::switch thumb translate → ~5%. **Biggest single CPU lever in the entire plan.**\n\n**Status:** in-progress (started 2026-05-24); P5.1 + P5.2 + P5.3 shipped\n**Estimate:** 3 weeks\n**Risk:** medium\n\n**Substep breakdown:**\n- 5.1 — Data structure + builder + tests (shipped). No consumer migration; foundation only.\n- 5.2 — Consumer migration (shipped). `apply_state_styles` + `apply_stylesheet_state_styles` consult the table when populated AND fresh against `build_generation`; fall back to rule-walk otherwise. Table is always empty today so behaviour is exactly preserved — P5.3 wires the build trigger and the win lands.\n- 5.3 — Build trigger + default-on (shipped). `set_stylesheet` / `set_stylesheet_arc` now auto-populate the table; freshness check relaxed to `is_populated()` only (stable-id contract handles per-node correctness across structural rebuilds). Rule-walk fallback retained for unbound-stylesheet / new-elements cases — full retirement deferred since the fallback is correct + cheap on empty-table queries.\n\n**Substep 5.2 — Consumer migration (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\n- New `state_style_table: RefCell\u003cStateStyleTable\u003e` field on `RenderTree`, initialised empty in `new()`. Phase 5.3 will mutate it on stylesheet-bind / class-set change.\n- Two private helpers on `RenderTree` (in `renderer/stylesheet/state.rs`) return `Option\u003cCow\u003c'a, ElementStyle\u003e\u003e`:\n  - `resolve_base_style(node, element_id, stylesheet)` — table-hit if `is_populated() \u0026\u0026 build_generation matches` (returns `Cow::Owned(style.clone())`); else `Cow::Borrowed(stylesheet.get(id))`.\n  - `resolve_state_style(node, element_id, state, stylesheet)` — same shape but for `:hover` / `:active` / `:focus` / `:disabled` / `:checked` lookups.\n- `apply_state_styles` migrated:\n  - All four direct stylesheet probes (transition_set extraction, base style, hover style, active/pressed style, focus style) replaced with `resolve_*` helpers.\n  - Critical correctness step: all four state lookups happen UP FRONT, before the `\u0026mut self.render_nodes` borrow. Cow values either hold a stylesheet borrow (lifetime independent of self) or an owned table clone — neither conflicts with the subsequent mutation pass.\n- `apply_stylesheet_state_styles` migrated for both `has_hover_anim` / `base_has_anim` checks. Reverted to `.as_deref().is_some_and(|s| s.animation.is_some())` instead of `.and_then(|s| s.animation.clone())` so the Cow is peeked without cloning a CssAnimation — matches the pre-migration zero-cost shape exactly.\n- 4 new tests in `p5_2_table_consumer_tests` (in `renderer/stylesheet/state.rs`) discriminate the two paths by building the table from a DIFFERENT stylesheet than the one bound to the tree (same id, distinct values), then asserting which value ends up on `render_node.props`:\n  1. `fallback_path_runs_when_table_empty` — bound stylesheet wins\n  2. `table_path_wins_when_populated_and_fresh` — table wins\n  3. `stale_table_generation_falls_back_to_stylesheet` — table with mismatching `build_generation` is ignored, fallback wins\n  4. `table_path_resolves_hover_state` — hover-state table entry is correctly layered on top of the base\n- 698 blinc_layout tests + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + workspace rustdoc clean.\n\n**Measured (Substep 5.2):** unmeasured — table is never populated in production yet, so every lookup hits the fallback path and behaviour / CPU shape is identical to pre-migration. The Cow-based machinery adds at most an `Option\u003cusize\u003e` discriminant in the borrow case — negligible. The win lands in P5.3 once `StateStyleTable::build` is invoked on stylesheet-bind.\n\n**Trade-off accepted:** the table-hit path clones `ElementStyle` (~few hundred bytes, includes Vec/Option/Color). For typical cn rules this is bounded by ~50 elements × 4 states = 200 clones per frame in the hot hover-storm scenario. That's still vastly cheaper than the ~25k string comparisons per frame the table replaces (rule walk through ~500 cn rules per registered element). Zero-cost-on-table-hit would require storing values as `Arc\u003cElementStyle\u003e` (cheap refcount clone); deferred — not blocking P5.3.\n\n**Substep 5.3 — Build trigger + default-on (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\n- New `RenderTree::rebuild_state_style_table(\u0026mut self)` public method:\n  - No stylesheet bound → clears the table (fallback rule walk runs).\n  - Stylesheet bound → walks `element_registry.all_ids()`, resolves each id to its `StableNodeId` via `layout_to_stable`, calls `StateStyleTable::build(stylesheet, elements, build_generation)`, installs the populated table.\n- Wired at the two binding entry points:\n  - `set_stylesheet(...)` — calls `rebuild_state_style_table()` after stamping `self.stylesheet`.\n  - `set_stylesheet_arc(...)` — same.\n- **Freshness check relaxed.** `resolve_base_style` / `resolve_state_style` (P5.2 helpers) now just check `is_populated()`; the strict `build_generation` equality check is dropped.\n  - Rationale: the stable-id contract preserves per-node correctness across structural rebuilds. Surviving nodes keep their `StableNodeId`, so table entries remain valid even after a Stateful rebuild bumps `build_generation`. Missing entries (newly added nodes) fall through to the rule walk correctly. Orphaned entries (removed nodes) are never queried. The table is only ever fully invalidated by a stylesheet rebind — and that path runs the rebuild eagerly.\n  - The `build_generation` stamp on `StateStyleTable` is retained as debugging metadata.\n- **Why no eager rebuild on Stateful structural rebuilds:** the relaxed freshness check means table entries for surviving nodes stay correct. New nodes pay one rule walk on their first lookup (fallback) and then live with that cost forever — acceptable until a measurement shows otherwise. A future P5.3b could add a surgical \"rebuild this node's row\" call into the subtree-rebuild path; deferred.\n- 2 new tests added to `p5_2_table_consumer_tests`:\n  - `set_stylesheet_auto_populates_table` — proves the auto-build trigger fires.\n  - `auto_populated_table_makes_apply_state_styles_consult_it` — proves end-to-end wiring stays glued (apply consults the auto-built table).\n- 1 P5.2 test replaced: `stale_table_generation_falls_back_to_stylesheet` → `set_stylesheet_rebinds_clear_stale_table_entries` (asserts the new policy: rebind rebuilds, so stale entries from an old stylesheet are never observed).\n- 714 blinc_layout tests + cn_demo release build clean + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + workspace rustdoc clean.\n\n**Measured (Substep 5.3 — initial bad capture):** captured `baselines/20260524-211422Z-after-p5-3.json` showed p95 **+30.7%**, mean **+25%** vs the post-P3.1a baseline. **Regression — not a win.**\n\n**Root cause:** `windowed.rs:5479` calls `tree.set_stylesheet_arc(stylesheet.clone())` on every incremental-update frame (i.e. ~every frame in cn_demo). My P5.3 made `set_stylesheet_arc` unconditionally trigger `rebuild_state_style_table`, so the entire ~500-rule cn cascade was rebuilt for ~150 registered elements **per frame**. Each rebuild iterates `element_registry.all_ids()` × resolves each id to stable_id × does 6 stylesheet probes (base + 5 states) × clones matching `ElementStyle`. Easily ~1ms/frame = ~60ms/s = the regression budget.\n\n**Fix (shipped immediately after the bad capture):** add `Arc::ptr_eq` guard inside `set_stylesheet_arc` — same Arc → skip rebuild. `set_stylesheet(Stylesheet)` (which allocates a fresh Arc) always rebuilds. New test `set_stylesheet_arc_skips_rebuild_for_same_pointer` locks the guard in. 715 blinc_layout tests + workspace clippy `-D warnings` clean.\n\n**Why this is safe:** the only way to change the stylesheet's *content* is to allocate a new Arc — either via `set_stylesheet(Stylesheet)` (always new Arc) or by re-parsing CSS (which produces a fresh Arc). Re-binding the same Arc means same content → same table → skip rebuild. The structural-rebuild case (new elements added with same stylesheet) is intentionally NOT triggered by the guard; new elements pay one rule-walk via fallback on first lookup, which is the correct behaviour-preserving outcome until a future P5.3b adds a surgical \"rebuild this node's row\" call.\n\n**Post-fix capture (`20260524-213038Z-after-p5-3-fix.json`):** p95 +52.8%, mean +167%, p50 0→2.07% vs post-P3.1a baseline — looked like an even worse regression. User pushback: Activity Monitor showed pre-P5 also spiked to ~30% on interactions, so the `compare` aggregates are misleading. Captures got progressively shorter (8min → 5min → 3min), and gesture-denser captures naturally pull mean/p50/p95 up even when per-interaction CPU shape is unchanged.\n\n**Honest verdict on P5:** the substep is **neutral, not a measurable win**. The v2 plan projected 20-40% from P5 assuming the rule walk was O(N) per stylesheet rule. It's not — `Stylesheet::get` for ID-based rules is already a single HashMap probe. The table replaces O(1) with O(1) at the cost of extra plumbing (stable_id lookup, RefCell borrow, ElementStyle clone on table-hit) — at best a wash, at worst a small floor increase masked by gesture variance.\n\n**Substep stays shipped because:**\n- The Arc::ptr_eq guard is a permanent fix regardless (per-frame rebuild was a real cost).\n- The substrate (table + consumer plumbing + lifecycle) is reusable for future per-property routing — a class-based extension, P9 entity-table migration, etc.\n- Behaviour-preserving by design: rule walk fallback handles all cases the table doesn't.\n\n**Headline cn_demo wins live elsewhere.** P4 (subtree-as-texture, projected toast 40→6%), P8 (animated layout scaling, projected slider 18→7%), P6 (visual-settle / deadline scheduler, projected switch tail 11→5%) target actually-O(N) hotspots — these are the substeps that will move the cn_demo numbers visibly.\n\n**Measurement methodology lesson** (saved for future captures): `cargo run -p blinc-regression -- compare` over captures with significantly different durations / gesture sets produces misleading deltas. Either match capture duration + gesture sequence exactly, or supplement with Activity Monitor side-channel verification. The chain's per-phase checklist should standardize gesture cadence too.\n\n**Substep 5.1 — Foundation (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\n- New `blinc_layout::state_style_table::StateStyleTable` module:\n  - `HashMap\u003cStableNodeId, ElementStyle\u003e` for base (`#id { ... }`) lookups\n  - `HashMap\u003c(StableNodeId, ElementState), ElementStyle\u003e` for stateful (`#id:hover`, `#id:active`, etc.) lookups\n  - `build_generation: u64` watermark for staleness detection by future consumers\n  - `populated: bool` flag — consumers treat empty / unpopulated as \"no table, use rule-walk fallback\"\n- `StateStyleTable::build(stylesheet, elements, build_generation)` walks each registered element + the five `ElementState` variants and pre-resolves the cascade once. Id-based rules only — class-based rules (`.cn-button:hover`) flow through the separate `apply_complex_selector_styles` path and aren't pre-resolved here yet (P5.2 will fold them in).\n- 8 unit tests cover empty stylesheet, no registered elements, single-state, all-states-for-one-node, multiple-elements, unmatched id, clear semantics, build-generation watermark passthrough. All pass.\n- Public surface: re-exported via `blinc_layout::StateStyleTable`.\n- 686 blinc_layout tests + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + rustdoc clean.\n\n**Substep 5.2 — Consumer migration (next)**\n\nMake `apply_state_styles` and `apply_stylesheet_state_styles` consult the table when present:\n- If `table.is_populated() \u0026\u0026 table.build_generation() == self.build_generation`: replace `stylesheet.get(\u0026element_id)` / `stylesheet.get_with_state(...)` calls with `table.get_base(stable_id)` / `table.get_state(stable_id, state)`.\n- Class-based selector path (`apply_complex_selector_styles`) remains as-is — P5.2 doesn't change it. P5.3 may consolidate.\n- Fallback: if table is empty or stale, the existing rule-walk path runs.\n- Same `apply_element_style_to_props` / `apply_element_style_to_taffy` machinery — only the lookup changes. No behavioural diff expected; correctness validated by cn_demo hover storms producing identical visuals.\n\n**Substep 5.3 — Build/invalidate triggers + default-on**\n\n- Build the table on `set_stylesheet_arc()` after the stylesheet is bound.\n- Invalidate on class-set change for affected nodes (lightweight — rebuild that node's row, not the whole table).\n- Drop the rule-walk fallback once the table-based path is stable.\n- Measure cn_demo hover-storm scenarios before/after to validate the 20-40% interaction-CPU envelope projection.\n\n**Measured (Substep 5.1):** unmeasured — table is built but unused; no CPU change expected. Wins land in 5.2 / 5.3.\n\n- `StateStyleTable: HashMap\u003c(StableNodeId, ElementState), Vec\u003c(PropertyId, Value)\u003e\u003e`.\n- Built at stylesheet-bind + on class-set change for affected nodes only.\n- `apply_stylesheet_state_styles` becomes table lookup + queue push, no per-frame stylesheet walk.\n- Cache invalidation: stylesheet hot-reload rebuilds; class change rebuilds affected nodes.\n\n**Risk drivers:** CSS cascade specificity + `!important` semantics must be exactly preserved by pre-resolution.\n**Win:** State-heavy interactions drop 20-40% during interaction. Combined with Phase 2 → hover storm goes through table → queue → patch in sub-frame time.\n\n**Status:** not started\n**Estimate:** 4 weeks\n**Risk:** low\n\n- Per-binding \"value changed at perceptible threshold\" check at tick (sub-pixel position, 1/256 colour, 1° rotation). Skip queue push if unchanged.\n- Spring \"visually settled\" detection separate from physical-settle. Physics state stays for replay; ticking stops.\n- AnimationRegistry deadline-bucketed: per-tick walk = O(currently-due) not O(all-registered).\n\n**Risk drivers:** Threshold tuning — too aggressive causes janky slow animations. Conservative thresholds avoid that.\n**Win:** Switch tail 11% → ~6% (after Phase 4+6). Spinner floor 9% → ~6%. Long-tail springs zero-cost post-settle.\n\n**Status:** not started\n**Estimate:** 4 weeks\n**Risk:** high\n\n- Static cache tiled by viewport-vertical strips (~256px).\n- Per-tile dirty tracking.\n- Scroll offset change invalidates only newly-visible tiles.\n- Refactor paint code that assumes monolithic cache (walker, render_static_layer_damaged, motion compositor).\n\n**Risk drivers:** Cache geometry refactor touches everything that reads the static cache; clip-rect math threading; viewport-cull interaction.\n**Win:** Scroll 25% → ~5%. Scrolling becomes CPU-free except for strip-shifted area.\n\n**Status:** in-progress (started 2026-05-24); P8.1 + P8.1b shipped\n**Estimate:** 2 weeks\n**Risk:** low\n\n**Substep breakdown:**\n- 8.1 — `.transform_width(signal)` + `.bind_transform_from(state, mapper)` + `.transform_origin(x, y)` builders on `Div`. Migrate `cn::progress(value)` to accept `impl IntoReactive\u003cf32\u003e`.\n- 8.1b — Single-input transform sugars + Derived ↔ IntoReactive bridge (shipped 2026-05-25). Makes `.scale` / `.rotate` / `.rotate_deg` accept `impl IntoReactive\u003cf32\u003e` (eager stays zero-cost via blanket); adds `Computed\u003cT\u003e` wrapper in blinc_core + `IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e for \u0026Computed\u003cT\u003e` impl + the notification path that fires bindings when a derived's value changes. Unblocks multi-axis reactivity via `ctx.use_computed(|g| Transform::translate(g.get(x)?, g.get(y)?))`.\n- 8.2 — `.animated_height_fraction(signal)` + `.animated_scale_x(signal)` siblings. Migrate cn::tabs underline + cn::accordion panel expand.\n\n**Substep 8.1 — transform_width + cn::progress reactive (shipped 2026-05-24)**\n\n**Surprise during exploration:** the v2 plan listed cn::slider AND cn::progress as P8.1 migration targets. Reading the code first revealed both were already GPU-transform-based:\n- `cn::slider` fill uses `motion().translate_x(thumb_offset)` on a full-width fill bar clipped by a parent track ([slider.rs:481](crates/blinc_cn/src/components/slider.rs#L481)).\n- `cn::progress_animated(SharedAnimatedValue)` uses the same translate-with-clipping pattern ([progress.rs:351](crates/blinc_cn/src/components/progress.rs#L351)).\n- `cn::progress(f32)` (static variant) bakes the fill width at build time — no per-frame layout cost EXCEPT when the value changes, which requires a Stateful rebuild of the entire progress subtree.\n\nSo the actual P8.1 target became: **make `cn::progress(value)` accept `impl IntoReactive\u003cf32\u003e` so a signal can drive it without Stateful rebuilds.** Bound path uses the new `.bind_transform_from` primitive to map `0..=100` → `Transform::scale(f/100, 1.0)` with `transform_origin(0, 50)`. Const path keeps the legacy width-baked path for zero per-frame cost on simple `cn::progress(75.0)` calls.\n\n**What shipped:**\n\n- `Div::transform_origin(x_percent, y_percent)` — per-element transform origin in percentages. `(0, 50)` pivots at the left edge mid-height (needed for left-anchored scale-x).\n- `Div::transform_width(impl IntoReactive\u003cf32\u003e)` — sugar for left-anchored scale-x. Const → static transform; Bound → subscribes to the signal, patches `props.transform` via the unified property channel each set, no `compute_layout` call, no Stateful rebuild.\n- `Div::bind_transform_from\u003cT\u003e(state: State\u003cT\u003e, mapper: impl Fn(T) -\u003e Transform)` — the underlying primitive, exposed publicly because cn::progress needs to map `0..=100` → `Transform::scale(f/100, 1.0)`. Generalises to any `State\u003cT\u003e` whose value derives a transform.\n- New `transform_origin: Option\u003c[f32; 2]\u003e` field on `Div` (was already on `RenderProps`, just no builder). Propagated through `render_props()`.\n- `cn::progress(impl IntoReactive\u003cf32\u003e)` — backward-compatible signature thanks to `impl IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e for T` blanket. `cn::progress(75.0)` still works; `cn::progress(\u0026state)` is the new reactive form. `ProgressConfig.value` is now `Reactive\u003cf32\u003e`; const branch keeps the static-width indicator, bound branch renders a full-width indicator with scale-bind.\n\n**Tests (4 new in `binding::tests` + 1 in `cn::progress::tests`):**\n- `div_transform_origin_propagates_to_render_props` — builder sets the field.\n- `div_transform_width_const_sets_scale_and_left_origin` — scale matrix populated, origin pinned to `(0, 50)`.\n- `div_transform_width_bound_fires_partial_update_on_set` — signal-set queues a `render_write` (not layout_write), `needs_layout == false`, applying the closure writes `Transform::scale(f, 1.0)` to `props.transform`.\n- `div_bind_transform_from_maps_arbitrary_signal_to_transform` — `State\u003cf32\u003e` with `0..=100 → scale(f/100, 1.0)` mapper; initial value seeded into `render_props().transform`, subsequent sets pipe through the mapper.\n- `test_progress_bound_value_is_reactive_bound` — passing `\u0026State\u003cf32\u003e` produces `Reactive::Bound` config so the build path takes the GPU branch.\n\n719 blinc_layout + 118 blinc_cn tests + cn_demo release build + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + workspace rustdoc clean.\n\n**Measured (Substep 8.1):** unmeasured here — capture `--label after-p8-1` over a cn_demo session that exercises the new reactive cn::progress (e.g. wire it to a slider or a slow-incrementing State\u003cf32\u003e). With Level 0 phase markers shipped, the per-phase delta for `progress-animated` / `slider-drag` scenarios should be duration-invariant for the first time.\n\n**Deferred to P8.2:**\n- `.animated_height_fraction(signal)` / `.animated_scale_x(signal)` siblings — same pattern as `transform_width`, just along different axes / without the unit normalisation.\n- cn::tabs underline migration (animated `left` + `width`).\n\n**Substep 8.1b — Single-input transform sugars + Derived ↔ IntoReactive bridge (shipped 2026-05-25)**\n\nTwo related landings in one substep:\n\n**(a) Single-input transform sugars reactive.** `.scale(impl IntoReactive\u003cf32\u003e)`, `.rotate(impl IntoReactive\u003cf32\u003e)`, `.rotate_deg(impl IntoReactive\u003cf32\u003e)` now accept eager OR signal-bound input. Eager call sites stay zero-cost via the blanket `IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e for T`. Bound paths flow through `bind_transform_from` with the appropriate mapper. Multi-input sugars (`.translate(x, y)`, `.scale_xy`) stay eager-only — the user composes via `.transform(\u0026State\u003cTransform\u003e)` (or now via `\u0026Computed\u003cTransform\u003e` — see (b)).\n\n**(b) Derived ↔ IntoReactive bridge.** Closes the multi-axis reactivity gap discovered while doing (a). Now `ctx.use_computed(|g| Transform::translate(g.get(x)?, g.get(y)?))` returns a `Computed\u003cTransform\u003e` that can drive any reactive `Div` builder directly.\n\nWhat shipped:\n\n- New `Computed\u003cT\u003e` wrapper in `blinc_core::reactive` — bundles `Derived\u003cT\u003e` (handle) with `SharedReactiveGraph`. Same shape as `State\u003cT\u003e`. Has `try_get` / `get` / `derived` / `derived_id` / `graph()` / `clone()`.\n- New global notifier hook `set_derived_binding_notifier(impl Fn(DerivedId))` + `notify_property_bindings_for_derived(id)`. Parallels the existing signal notifier.\n- `ReactiveGraph::derived_dirty_buffer` accumulates derived ids that flipped from clean to dirty during a `set()`-driven dirty propagation walk. New `take_dirty_derived()` accessor drains the buffer.\n- **Deadlock fix that was the headline correctness issue:** `State::set` / `set_rebuild` / `update` / `update_rebuild` now:\n  1. Acquire the graph lock.\n  2. Call `graph.set(...)` (or `update`).\n  3. Call `graph.take_dirty_derived()` to drain the buffer.\n  4. **Drop the lock.**\n  5. Fire `notify_property_bindings` (signal-side, existing).\n  6. Fire `notify_property_bindings_for_derived(d_id)` for each dirty derived (new).\n  Firing inline INSIDE the lock would deadlock because the binding registry's read closures call `Computed::try_get` which re-acquires the same mutex. Discovered the hard way — multiple test runs hanging until the fix landed.\n- `PropertyBindingRegistry` extended with `derived_bindings: HashMap\u003cDerivedId, Vec\u003cSubscriber\u003e\u003e` + `derived_by_node: HashMap\u003cLayoutNodeId, Vec\u003cDerivedId\u003e\u003e` reverse index. `register_derived` / `register_derived_layout` / `fire_derived` / `unregister_node` (extended). `dispatch_subscribers` helper factored out of `fire` so both signal + derived paths share the queue-partial-update logic.\n- New `Reactive::Computed(Computed\u003cT\u003e)` variant on the `Reactive\u003cT\u003e` enum. `IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e` impls for `\u0026Computed\u003cT\u003e` and `Computed\u003cT\u003e`. Every existing reactive builder in `div.rs` (14 sites) + `cn::progress` gained a `Reactive::Computed` arm that mirrors the `Reactive::Bound` arm with `state` → `computed` and `TypedPendingBinding::new` → `TypedPendingBinding::from_computed`.\n- `TypedPendingBinding\u003cT\u003e` and `LayoutPendingBinding\u003cT\u003e` refactored to internally hold a `BindingSource\u003cT\u003e { Signal(State\u003cT\u003e) | Derived(Computed\u003cT\u003e) }` enum. `register()` dispatches on the source kind. Keeps the single `Box\u003cdyn PendingBinding\u003e` shape on the builder side; no new struct names to import.\n- `Div::bind_transform_from` extended to accept `impl IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e` — handles Const (compute eagerly), Bound (existing path), Computed (new path) inside one match.\n- New context-layer hook: `Stateful::StateContext::use_computed(|g| ...)` + free `blinc_core::context_state::use_computed(|g| ...)` + method on `BlincContextState`. Each mints a fresh `Derived` on every call (no keyed-persistence-across-rebuilds yet — a future `use_computed_keyed` substep).\n\nTests (8 new total — 5 in single-input sugars + 3 in derived bridge):\n- `div_scale_const_path_unchanged`, `div_scale_bound_path_fires_partial_update_on_set`, `div_rotate_const_path_unchanged`, `div_rotate_bound_path_fires_partial_update_on_set`, `div_rotate_deg_bound_path_converts_degrees_to_radians`.\n- `computed_into_reactive_produces_computed_variant` — `\u0026Computed\u003cf32\u003e` resolves to `Reactive::Computed(_)`.\n- `computed_bound_transform_fires_on_dep_signal_set` — headline test. Two signals `x`, `y` combined into a `Derived\u003cTransform\u003e` via auto-tracking. `div().transform(\u0026computed)`. Setting `x` fires one partial update with the recomputed `Transform::translate(100, 20)`; setting `y` fires another with `translate(100, 200)`. **Multi-source reactivity works end-to-end.**\n- `computed_unregister_node_drops_derived_subscriber` — `unregister_node` evicts from BOTH the signal map and the derived map; later signal sets don't queue updates for the removed node.\n\n930 total tests (727 blinc_layout + 85 blinc_core + 118 blinc_cn) + workspace clippy `-D warnings` + workspace rustdoc clean.\n\n**Why this unlocks multi-axis transforms without changing `.translate` / `.scale_xy`:**\n\n```rust\nlet x = ctx.use_state(0.0_f32);\nlet y = ctx.use_state(0.0_f32);\nlet pos = ctx.use_computed(move |g| {\n    Transform::translate(g.get(x.signal())?, g.get(y.signal())?)\n});\ndiv().transform(\u0026pos)  // composes naturally through the existing reactive .transform\n```\n\nThe eager multi-input `.translate(x, y)` / `.scale_xy(sx, sy)` keep their signatures — they're still useful for static cases. Reactive composition is one extra `use_computed` line but reads cleanly and benefits from the whole graph's auto-tracking + cycle detection.\n\n**Future work:**\n- `use_computed_keyed` for persistence across Stateful rebuilds (parity with `use_state_keyed` / `use_signal_keyed`).\n- \"skip notify if value unchanged\" optimisation — read prev/current and bail if equal. Probably worth measuring before adding; the registry walk is already cheap.\n- cn::accordion panel expand (animated `height`).\n- cn::slider stays on the existing `motion().translate_x` path — it's already optimal.\n- cn::progress_animated stays on `motion().translate_x` — same.\n\n**Risk drivers:** hit-testing on scaled elements works because the layout box stays full-size; user code that programmatically reads bounds (`get_bounds`) gets the unscaled layout values, which is the intended behaviour for the progress-fill-style use case. Document the trade-off if a consumer needs scaled bounds.\n\n**Status:** not started\n**Estimate:** 8 weeks\n**Risk:** very high\n\n- Per-property `SparseSet\u003cLayoutNodeId, T\u003e` stores replacing `RenderProps` struct.\n- Subsystem migration order: (a) animation tickers, (b) CSS state table writes, (c) compositor delta reads, (d) walker/paint emit (last, hardest).\n- `RenderProps`-shaped transient view assembled per-node before primitive emit (mitigates walker cache-locality regression).\n\n**Risk drivers:** Massive refactor; many sites. Walker regression mitigation must be measured carefully.\n**Win:** Memory ~3x reduction on sparsely-overridden trees. Iteration-heavy paths (animation tick, CSS resolution) ~2-3x faster on dense-animation scenes. Negligible win on single-animation cases.\n**Only do if P1-8 measurement justifies cost.**\n\n---\n\n\n| Scenario | Today | After P1-3 | After P4-6 | After P7-8 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Toast hide/show | 40% | 38% | **6%** | 6% |\n| Scroll + momentum | 25% | 22% | 22% | **5%** |\n| Slider drag | 18% | **13%** | 11% | **7%** |\n| cn::switch toggle | 11% | 10% | **5%** | 5% |\n| Spinner @ 30fps | 13% | **9%** | **6%** | 6% |\n| Hover storm on `.cn-*` | ~30%* | ~10% (P2) | ~6% (+P5) | 5% |\n\n*Hover-storm CPU is estimated, not measured. Confirm during Phase 2 work.\n\n**Cumulative under simultaneous-interaction load:** 137% today → ~64% after P1-3 → ~46% after P4-6 → **~34% after P7-8.** Headroom for slider + scroll + animated content + concurrent CSS state changes without frame drops.\n\n---\n\n\n- **P1 → P2 are ordered and foundational.** Everything else assumes them.\n- **P3 next** (broad, small, ships value fast).\n- **P4 third** (biggest single lever).\n- **P5 / P6 / P7 / P8 roughly independent** of each other; pick by team capacity + biggest open hotspot in user feedback.\n- **P9 last** and only after P1-8 measurement justifies it.\n\n\nAfter P3.1a shipped + P3.2 turned out to be mostly already-built (infrastructure ready; default-on blocked on Metal stutter), the chain reorganised around a key insight: **P4 (subtree-as-texture) doing its job kills the P3.2 Metal stutter as a side effect.** Motion-bound subtrees leave the static cache; the partial-write pattern that causes the stutter just doesn't happen. P3.2-finalize collapses into a single-commit checkpoint after P4 lands.\n\nTwo interleaved tracks:\n\n**Track A — compositor architecture** (sequential, big arc):\n- P4 substepped like P1/P2:\n  - 4.1: Detection — walker marks subtree roots whose only animated props are transform/opacity AND no descendant has independent CSS animation / motion binding\n  - 4.2: Texture-baking infrastructure (offscreen render-pass scaffolding, GPU memory bookkeeping)\n  - 4.3: Bake-at-motion-start hook + per-frame texture-blit during motion\n  - 4.4: Invalidation triggers (structural rebuild, non-transform binding fires, descendant CSS animation toggles)\n  - 4.5: LRU eviction policy + memory cap\n- P3.2-finalize: re-enable `BLINC_DAMAGE_RECT` default, validate Metal stutter gone.\n\n**Track B — CPU wins on existing widgets** (interleaved with Track A):\n- P5 (CSS state-style table) — substep-able; independent of P4 (touches `apply_stylesheet_state_styles`, not compositor).\n- P8 (layout-prop animation helpers) — independent of P4; small landings (~2 weeks total).\n\n**Concrete interleaved sequence** (alternating substeps so Track B's compounding wins keep landing while Track A's big arc progresses):\n\n1. P5.1 ✓ — CSS state-table substep 1 (quick measurable win)\n2. P4.1 ✓ — Detection pass\n3. P5.2 ✓ — CSS state-table substep 2\n4. P4.2 ✓ — Texture-baking infrastructure\n5. P5.3 ✓ — CSS state-table finalize\n6. P8.1 ✓ — `.transform_width` helper + `cn::progress` reactive migration (slider already optimal — see substep notes)\n7. P4.3 — Bake at motion-start\n8. P8.2 — Migrate `cn::progress` + `cn::tabs` underline + `cn::accordion`\n9. P4.4 — Invalidation triggers\n10. P4.5 — LRU eviction + memory cap\n11. P3.2-finalize — Re-enable damage-rect default; validate\n12. P6 — Animation lifecycle (now motion subsystem is stable from P4)\n13. P7 — Tiled scroll cache\n14. P9 (optional) — Entity-table\n\nTotal: ~5 months for steps 1-11. P5 / P8 wins land every ~2 weeks; P4 milestones every ~1-2 weeks. If a Track A substep blocks (architectural surprise, GPU correctness issue), Track B keeps moving so momentum doesn't stall.\n\n**Checkpoint discipline per [[feedback-cn-demo-regression-bed]]**: after each numbered step, surface the relevant capture scenarios from the per-phase checklist; user re-records with `--label after-pN-M`; `compare` produces the delta. The before/after naming convention via `--label` makes the progression naturally trackable.\n\n**Rationale for the order:**\n- P5.1 first because it's a quick measurable win that compounds with P3.1a's p95 -19% — keeps the demonstrated-CPU-savings momentum visible.\n- P4 substeps anchored between P5 substeps so the big arc progresses but the user isn't waiting weeks between commits.\n- P8 woven into the second half because its migrations (slider / progress / tabs / accordion) need P4's texture-bake to fully cash in — `.scale_x` on the slider fill is most efficient when the motion-bound parent baking is also in play.\n- P6 deferred until P4 stabilises because P6's \"visual-settled detection\" interacts with motion-binding code that P4 reshapes.\n- P7 truly orthogonal; could slot in anywhere after P4 if scroll becomes the visible hotspot in measurements.\n\n---\n\n\n1. **`IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e` generic strategy.** Direct generic on every builder method vs. type-erased internal stub. Prototype on a branch to measure compile-time impact.\n2. **Texture cache size budget for P4.** Hard cap (LRU above N MB), proportional (% available GPU), or unbounded with motion-end eviction.\n3. **Pointer-move coalescing + drag-state snapshot semantics.** Audit drag-fast-path handlers for intermediate-position invariants.\n4. **CSS cascade pre-resolution interaction with inline `.bg(signal)` + `!important`.** Document the precedence rule before P2/P5 ship.\n5. **Tiled cache geometry for P7.** 256px assumed; tune against representative scroll content (long forms vs. infinite lists).\n\n---\n\n\n- Foundation: [[project-layout-tree-stability-substrate]]\n- Vizia comparison driving design choices: [[project-blinc-perf-vs-vizia]], [[reference-vizia-architecture]]\n- Older design (superseded by current substrate): [[project-stable-node-id-design]]\n- Motivating gotchas (each phase attacks one or more):\n  - [[gotcha-spinner-pinned-text-collect]] — P3 + P6\n  - [[gotcha-damage-rect-clears-text]] — P3\n  - [[gotcha-scrollbar-spring-spin]] — P6 (visual-settle)\n  - [[gotcha-canvas-vs-static-z-ordering]] — P7 (tiled cache may help/complicate)\n  - [[gotcha-fast-path-visible-anim-latch]] — P6 (lifecycle discipline)\n\n\n**Rule:** `examples/blinc_app_examples/examples/cn_demo.rs` is the single regression test bed for every phase. Before any phase work begins, capture a baseline; after the phase lands, re-run the same scenarios and compare. Each phase's win projection in the envelope table is *measured against cn_demo*, not against synthetic benchmarks.\n\n\n| cn_demo section | Phases that touch it most |\n|---|---|\n| `buttons_section` | P2 (signal-bound bg/color), P5 (state-style hover) |\n| `badges_section`, `cards_section`, `alerts_section` | P2, P5 |\n| `form_inputs_section` (input, textarea, number_input) | P2 (signal-bound value), P5 (focus state) |\n| `toggles_section` (toggle, toggle_group) | P2, P4 (transform), P6 (visual-settle) |\n| `slider_section` | **P3 (coalescing), P8 (scale_x fill)**, P2 |\n| `radio_section`, `select_section`, `combobox_section` | P2, P5 (dropdown hover), P4 (panel motion) |\n| `dialog_section`, `sheet_section`, `drawer_section` | **P4 (subtree-as-texture)**, P6 |\n| `loading_section`, `progress_section` | P6 (visual-settle), P8 (scale_x progress) |\n| `tabs_section` | P4 (transition), P8 (animated underline) |\n| `breadcrumb_section`, `pagination_section`, `navigation_menu_section` | P5 (state styles) |\n| `sidebar_section`, `resizable_section`, `scroll_area_section` | **P7 (tiled cache for scroll)** |\n| `aspect_ratio_section`, `avatar_section` | (minimal — static) |\n| `toast_section` | **P4 (subtree-as-texture)** — primary motivator |\n| `hover_card_section`, `popover_section`, `tooltip_section` | P4 (enter/exit), P5 (hover state) |\n| `kbd_section`, `icon_gallery_section`, `misc_section` | (minimal — static) |\n| `tree_view_section` | P4 (expand/collapse motion), P5 |\n| `table_section` | P5 (hover row), P7 (scroll if large) |\n| `charts_section` | P6 (canvas animation lifecycle if any) |\n| `accordion_section`, `context_menu_section`, `dropdown_menu_section` | P4 (open/close), P5 (item hover) |\n| `toast_section` revisit | P4, P6 |\n\n\nFor every phase, before starting:\n\n1. **Visual baseline.** Run cn_demo on main, screenshot each section in:\n   - resting state\n   - one interaction state (hovered / opened / dragged-mid / animation-mid)\n   - post-interaction settled state\n2. **CPU baseline.** Profile cn_demo during scripted interaction scenarios:\n   - idle (cn_demo open, no interaction) — should be near 0% with compositor fast path\n   - slider drag (P3, P8 target)\n   - toast open / close (P4 target)\n   - scroll in scroll_area / table (P7 target)\n   - switch toggle (P4, P6 target)\n   - hover storm — sweep mouse rapidly across `.cn-button` row (P2, P5 target)\n   - spinner steady-state (P6 target)\n   - dialog open / close (P4 target)\n   - simultaneous: slider drag + spinner + animated content (compound CPU)\n3. **Capture format.** Screenshots + CPU% timeseries per scenario, stamped with branch + commit SHA.\n\nAfter the phase lands:\n\n1. Re-run the same scenarios.\n2. Visual diff against baseline — any pixel difference outside the targeted sections is a regression to investigate.\n3. CPU comparison: each scenario should match or improve the projected envelope. Worse scenarios = regression to investigate or accept-and-document.\n4. Update the envelope table in this doc: replace the projected column with measured numbers.\n\n\nEach phase has secondary effects on sections it doesn't directly target. Watch for:\n\n- **P2 (signal-bound modifiers):** every cn component is touched (builder signatures change). Watchlist = all sections. Especially scrutinise compile time + run time of static (no-state) call sites — they MUST stay zero-cost.\n- **P3 (pointer coalescing):** drag-heavy sections (slider, sheet swipe, scroll, resizable). Verify drag intermediate-position invariants in resizable_section (where drag handlers may snapshot per-event).\n- **P4 (subtree-as-texture):** any motion-heavy section. Verify texture invalidation triggers fire correctly when descendant CSS animations / signal bindings coexist with parent transform. Watch tree_view (expand has both transform and content change).\n- **P5 (state-style table):** every section with hover / focus / disabled / checked. Verify CSS specificity preserved — especially in cn_demo's nested `.cn-button.cn-button--primary:hover`-style cascades.\n- **P6 (animation lifecycle):** all spring-driven sections. Watch slow animations (long-duration transitions, drag-release momentum) — threshold tuning may cut visible tail.\n- **P7 (tiled scroll cache):** scroll_area_section, sidebar_section, big lists. Verify motion-bound subtrees inside a scroll viewport still render correctly (toast in scroll, sticky overlay in scroll).\n- **P8 (transform helpers):** affected widgets only (slider, progress, tabs underline, accordion). Verify hit-testing still works on scale-transformed elements.\n\n\nWhen the user re-records baselines for a phase comparison, walk them through THIS checklist for the phase that just concluded (don't dump all phases at once). The scenarios catalogue in `tools/blinc-regression/src/main.rs` lists what to capture; this section lists *how* — the actual gestures.\n\n**Pre-flight (every run):**\n- Idle 30s untouched (must be first — establishes the compositor fast-path floor)\n\n**P2 — signal-bound modifiers**\n- Sweep mouse rapidly across cn::button row (~10s)\n- Sweep mouse up/down cn::table rows (~10s)\n- Drag a cn::slider thumb full-range ×5 (~10s)\n- Tab/click through form inputs to fire focus rings ×5\n- Hover over cn::switch, badges, alerts rows\n\n**P3 — pointer coalescing + damage-rect text/SVG**\n- Slider: slow continuous drag (~5s) then fast continuous drag (~5s) — coalescing wins are biggest on fast drags\n- cn::resizable splitter back-and-forth ~10s\n- Spinner running untouched ~30s (steady-state floor)\n\n**P4 — subtree-as-texture (transform/opacity-only motion)** — largest single lever\n- 5+ toasts in quick succession; let each auto-dismiss\n- cn::dialog open + close ×5\n- cn::drawer open + close ×5 (both sides if applicable)\n- cn::sheet open + close ×5 (each side)\n- cn::switch toggle ×5, **wait 1s between toggles** for spring decay\n- hover-card / popover / tooltip ~10s\n- Accordion expand + collapse ×3\n\n**P5 — CSS state-style table**\n- Hover sweep across navigation menu items\n- Hover sweep across sidebar items\n- Open + hover sweep across dropdown menu items (~5s)\n- Open + hover sweep across context menu items (~5s)\n- Open + hover sweep across cn::select and cn::combobox options\n- Click through cn::tabs triggers\n- Hover sweep across pagination buttons\n\n**P6 — animation lifecycle (visual-settle + deadline scheduler)**\n- Spinner running ~30s (overlaps with P3 — one capture suffices)\n- cn::switch toggle then wait 2s for spring tail ×3 (don't immediately re-toggle)\n- Progress bar full animation to completion\n- Toast triggered + left to auto-dismiss naturally ×3\n- Dialog open + wait + close — enter/hold/exit observable\n\n**P7 — tiled scroll cache**\n- Flick-scroll cn::scroll_area to trigger momentum ×5, let each decay\n- Scroll the table section up and down (~10s)\n- Sidebar scroll if applicable\n- Slow drag-scroll vs fast flick (different cost shapes)\n\n**P8 — layout-property animation helpers** (overlaps P2/P3)\n- Slider drag (P2) already exercises the fill\n- Progress bar animation (P6) exercises fill\n- Tab navigation (P5) — animated underline if any\n- Accordion expand (P4) — height animation\n\n**Compound — simultaneous interaction floor** (the \"real headroom\" test, always do as final segment)\n- Drag slider WHILE spinner animates (~10s)\n- Scroll WHILE hovering visible buttons (~10s)\n- Open dialog WHILE toast is still entering\n\n**Order (single-capture run):** Idle 30s → P2 hovers → P3 drags → P5 menu hovers → P6 spinner 30s → P4 overlays → P7 scroll → Compound. Total ~6-8 min, resist dwelling.\n\n**Sections to skip** (mostly static, no phase delta):\n- avatar gallery, kbd display, icon gallery, breadcrumb, aspect-ratio, charts (unless triggering canvas animations), card / alert containers.\n\n\nProperly running this requires (build alongside P1):\n\n- A CPU profiling helper that can stamp samples per-section / per-scenario in cn_demo. Likely a thin macro that wraps interaction-scoped code with `tracing` spans + a separate harness that runs scripted interactions and exports CPU% timeseries.\n- A visual diff harness (probably `image-compare` crate or similar) that runs cn_demo headless, captures screenshots, diffs against baselines.\n- Both should live in `tools/` or `examples/blinc_app_examples/regression/` and be invoked via a single `cargo run -p blinc-regression -- --phase \u003cN\u003e` command.\n\nThese aren't blockers for P1 but should land alongside P1's plumbing so subsequent phases have a measurement substrate.\n\n\nWhen a phase starts, mark **Status:** in-progress, link the PR / branch. On completion, mark **Status:** shipped with the commit SHA + the measured before/after CPU number (replacing the projected envelope row). Phases that get re-scoped should keep the original estimate and add a **Revised:** note.\n\nEach completed phase also updates:\n1. The envelope table — projected → measured.\n2. The phase's `Status:` field with the PR / commit ref.\n3. A short post-phase note listing any cn_demo sections that *did* regress and the tradeoff accepted, if any. This is how unexpected interactions surface for future phases.","files":null}]}